.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
These functions operate on
null-terminated
strings.  They do not check for overflow of
any receiving string.
.LP
.B str2bool(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if 
.I s
can be converted to a boolean value.  
The successful result is placed in
.IR b.
Otherwise,
.B str2bool(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2double(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if 
.I s
can be converted to a double value.  
The successful result is placed in
.IR d.
Otherwise,
.B str2double(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2float(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if 
.I s
can be converted to a floating point number.  
The successful result is placed in
.IR f.
Otherwise,
.B str2float(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2int(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if
.I s
can be converted to an integer.  The successful result is placed in
.IR i.
Otherwise,
.B str2int(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2lower(\|)
converts all upper cased letters in the string
.I s
to lower case and places the result in string
.I lower.
.LP
.B str2short(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if
.I s
can be converted to an short integer.  The successful result is placed in
.IR si.
Otherwise,
.B str2short(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2uint(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if
.I s
can be converted to an unsigned integer.  The successful result is placed in
.IR u.
Otherwise,
.B str2uint(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B str2upper(\|)
converts all lower cased letters in the string
.I s
to upper case and places the result in string
.I upper.
.LP
.B str2ushort(\|)
evaluates the argument
.I s
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if
.I s
can be converted to an unsigned short integer.  The successful result is placed in
.IR u.
Otherwise,
.B str2ushort(\|) 
returns 
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B streq(\|)
compares the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
and returns
.SM TRUE 
if the strings are identical.  Otherwise
.B streq(\|)
returns
.SM FALSE.
.LP
.B strapp(\|)
Appends string 
.I s2
to string
.I s1
reallocating memory as necessary.  
.I s1
and
.I s2
must be null terminated strings.  The string returned is always null
terminated.  
.SM NULL 
is returned in the event of a memory allocation error.
.LP
.B strext(\|)
accepts a pointer to a string
.I pathname 
and returns a pointer to the suffix or extension of the filename.
The suffix is preceded by the last dot in the
.IR pathname.
If there is no suffix,
.B strext(\|) 
will return a 
.SM NULL 
pointer.
.LP
.B strfit(\|)
accepts a pointer to a NULL terminated string
.I s,
removes all leading and trailing white space and
returns the pointer to the modified string.
.LP
.B strfloat(\|)
verifies that a string
.I s
is a valid 
.SM ASCII 
representation of a floating point number, and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if that string is valid.  Note:
.B strfloat(\|)
will return true only for a string of digits with at most 1 decimal
point.  It will not validate scientific notation floats.
.LP
.B strhead(\|)
accepts a pointer to a string 
.I pathname
and modifies the string to include only the path of the directory
in which the file resides.  If no directory is specified in
.IR pathname,
it will remain unchanged.
.LP
.B strint(\|)
verifies that a string
.I s
is a valid 
.SM ASCII 
representation of an integer.  
.B strint(\|)
returns 
.SM TRUE 
if the string is valid, and 
.SM FALSE 
otherwise.
.LP
.B strnapp(\|)
Appends string 
.I s2
to string
.I s1
copying a maximum of 
.I l
characters, and reallocating memory if necessary.  
.I s1
and
.I s2
must be null terminated strings.  The string returned is always null
terminated.  
.SM NULL 
is returned in the event of a memory allocation error.
.LP
.B strne(\|)
compares the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
and returns
.SM FALSE
if the strings are identical.  Otherwise
.B strne(\|)
returns
.SM TRUE.
.LP
.B strnsav(\|)
allocates memory for a string
.I s
of maximum size
.I l
and copies the string to that location.
.B strnsav(\|)
returns a pointer to the copied string or a 
.SM NULL 
pointer in the case of a memory allocation error.  The returned string
is always null terminated.
.LP
.B strpattern(\|)
compares a string
.I source 
with a string
.I pattern
and returns 
.SM TRUE 
if the information in the 
.I pattern 
string occurs in the 
.I source.
.LP
.B strroot(\|)
accepts a pointer to a string
.I pathname
and modifies that string, removing the suffix from the filename. 
The suffix begins with the last dot in the string
.IR pathname.  
If there is no suffix in the path,
.I pathname
will remain unchanged.
.LP
.B strsav(\|)
copies the argument
.I s
and returns a pointer to the copy.  
If there is a memory allocation error
.B srtsav(\|) 
will return a
.SM NULL
pointer.
.LP
.B strtail(\|)
accepts a pointer to a string
.I pathname
and returns the basename of the path in 
.IR pathname.
The basename begins after the last `/' in the path.  If 
.I pathname 
has no `/' then the whole string is returned.
.LP 
.B strtokens(\|)
takes the argument
.I buffer
and separates that argument into tokens.  The tokens are identified as
being separated by a whitespace character.  Tokens containing white 
space can be entered by enclosing the white space in quotes (").
The resulting tokens are placed an array of strings identified
.I tokens
and the number of tokens removed from
.I buffer
is placed in 
.IR max_tokens.
The maximum number of tokens to be removed is delimited by
.IR max_tokens. 
Note:
.I buffer
is modified by this function.
.br
.nf
.sp
.SH NOTES
.LP
.B gen_str.h
includes
.B string.h.

.SH WARNINGS
.B streq(\|)
and
.B strne(\|)
use native character comparison and assume the
.I ASCII
character set.
.LP
On the Sun processor, as well as on many other machines, you can
.I not
use a
.SM NULL
pointer to indicate a
null string.  A
.SM NULL
pointer is an error and results in an abort
of the program.  If you wish to indicate a
null string, you must have a pointer that
points to an explicit
null string.  On some implementations of the C
language on some machines, a
.SM NULL
pointer, if dereferenced, would yield a
null string; this highly
non-portable trick was used in some programs.
Programmers using a
.SM NULL
pointer to represent an empty string should be
aware of this portability issue; even on
machines where dereferencing a
.SM NULL
pointer does not cause an abort of the
program, it does not necessarily yield a
null string.
.nf
.sp
